# Workflow on nicodesktop (autonomous mode)

## Environment

- Windows, PowerShell, workspace `C:\Users\nico\Desktop\stock_overflow_workspace`, data
  `C:\Users\nico\Desktop\stock_overflow_data\` (outside the workspace; read it with Python, never copy it into the repo).
- Python: always `venv-main\Scripts\python.exe` (the workspace is installed in it in editable mode, so `so` and
  `experiments` import from anywhere).
- Packages: never upgrade or remove an installed package (it could change past results). If a new package is truly
  needed, install a pinned version into `venv-main` only, add it to the requirements file, and record it in the results
  log.
- Tests: `venv-main\Scripts\python.exe tests\run_all_tests.py` (a few minutes). Run them after every code change and
  before every commit. All suites must pass; never commit failing tests.
- The data folder has **no** Windows protection (Nicolas removed it on 2026-10-06 and keeps a backup): nothing technical
  stops a delete or an overwrite, so the "File scope" rules of `research-integrity.mdc` are the only protection. Before
  any command that deletes, moves or overwrites something in the data folder, check that every target is something you
  created under `store04_experiments\` (never `trial_log.csv`, which is only appended to through `so.core.trial_log`).
  An "access denied" anywhere in the data folder: stop that action, do not work around it, and record it.

## Git

- Work on the branch `agent/research` (create it from `main` the first time: `git switch -c agent/research`; later
  `git switch agent/research`). Never commit to `main`; Nicolas merges after review.
- Commit after every meaningful step (pre-registration, code + tests, each executed notebook with its outputs, each
  results-log entry). At the end of a session: `git push -u origin agent/research` (never `--force`).

## Running notebooks

- Notebooks are run top to bottom on a fresh kernel, never cell by cell, so that the saved outputs are the evidence:

  ```
  venv-main\Scripts\jupyter.exe nbconvert --to notebook --execute --inplace --ExecutePreprocessor.timeout=-1 --ExecutePreprocessor.kernel_name=venv-main <notebook path>
  ```

  If `venv-main\Scripts\jupyter.exe` does not exist, park it as a decision and use `venv-main\Scripts\python.exe -m
  jupyter nbconvert ...` if that works.
- Runs longer than a few minutes (exp01-style walk-forwards take ~1 h): start them in the background with a log and poll
  the log instead of blocking the terminal:

  ```
  New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force logs | Out-Null
  Start-Process -FilePath venv-main\Scripts\jupyter.exe -ArgumentList 'nbconvert','--to','notebook','--execute','--inplace','--ExecutePreprocessor.timeout=-1','--ExecutePreprocessor.kernel_name=venv-main','<notebook path>' -RedirectStandardOutput logs\<name>.out -RedirectStandardError logs\<name>.err -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru
  Get-Content logs\<name>.err -Tail 5
  ```

  The run is finished when the `.err` log ends with `Writing ... bytes to <notebook>`; a Python error appears there too.
  While it runs, work on something that does not depend on it (documentation, tests, the next protocol).
- Read results from the saved notebook outputs and from `store04_experiments\<experiment>\...` CSV files with Python.
- Each notebook warns when the trial log already holds entries of its stage: a re-run ADDS trials. Do not re-run a
  validation notebook unless the protocol requires it; if a run crashed half-way, fix the cause, record the crash and the
  trials it already logged in the results log, then re-run once.

## Recording (same session as the run)

1. `docs/RESULTS_LOG.md`: date, experiment and step, protocol version, config hash (printed in the header cell), trials
   added, headline numbers copied from the outputs, what they support and what they do not, status (CONTINUE / STOP).
   Explain the key result both mathematically and in plain words.
2. The experiment's `PROTOCOL.md` change log if anything changed; `experiments/README.md` registry status; root `README.md`
   status table when an experiment starts or ends.
3. Copy `C:\Users\nico\Desktop\stock_overflow_data\store04_experiments\trial_log.csv` to `records\trial_log_snapshot.csv`.
4. Commit with a message that says what was run and the outcome (e.g. `exp04 step02 validation: prior-only +X% vs +Y%,
   STOP`).

## Building an experiment

Follow `experiments/README.md` ("Adding an experiment") and copy the structure of `experiments/exp02_stop_reentry/` (the
reference walk-forward) or `experiments/exp03_ath_exit/` (rules without a model). Reuse `so.core` (schedule,
simulators, evaluation, trial log). Shared helpers go to `so/core` or `so/features` with tests in `tests/`, without
changing existing behaviour (the reproduction checks of exp01-exp03 in `docs/RESULTS_LOG.md` must still hold). New test
files are added to `tests\run_all_tests.py`.

## Working loop

1. Start of a chat: read `AGENTS.md`, the latest `docs/RESEARCH_STATE_*.md` (roadmap, open items, parked decisions,
   session log) and `docs/RESULTS_LOG.md`; check `git status` and the branch.
2. Take the next roadmap item that is not blocked. Write a short plan in the chat (files, functions, tests, expected
   trials), then do it: protocol → code and tests → pre-registration commit → runs → recording → commit.
3. When a methodological choice is open, write the options and their trade-offs in the protocol, choose the most
   conservative one (the one least likely to flatter the result), and say so. Never choose the option that gives the
   best-looking result.
4. Repeat until the roadmap is done or every remaining item is blocked by a parked decision.

## Ending a session

Append one line to §8 of the latest `docs/RESEARCH_STATE_*.md` (date, what was done, next step), update its open items
and parked decisions, make sure everything is committed and pushed to `agent/research`, and summarize in the chat: what
was run, the outcomes, the trials added, the parked decisions, and the next step.
